bodies-in-liquids-build-question = A clear bottle of { $what } with marks every 3 cm. We turn it over and watch a small air bubble (≈ 2 mm): start the stopwatch as it passes the zero mark and note the time at 3, 6, 9 and 12 cm. Then the same with a bigger bubble (≈ 4 mm). Does a bubble’s speed depend on its size?
bodies-in-liquids-answer-bubble-speed-why = A bubble feels the buoyant force (up) and the thick liquid’s drag (down). While it speeds up, the drag grows — and in a moment matches the buoyant force. From then on the forces balance and the bubble rises at a steady speed — uniformly: equal paths in equal times. A bigger bubble pushes aside much more liquid (the buoyant force grows as the cube of its size), the drag only in proportion to its size and speed. So it needs more speed for the drag to match the buoyancy: a bubble twice as big rises about four times as fast.
bodies-in-liquids-answer-bubble-speed-say = The bigger bubble rose ≈ { $ratio } times as fast!
bodies-in-liquids-answer-bubble-speed-say-2 = Let us look at the stopwatch.
bodies-in-liquids-answer-bubble-speed-text = A ≈ { $d } mm bubble: { $p }
bodies-in-liquids-answer-bubble-speed-text-2 = {" "}(How fast exactly depends on the liquid: makes are several times thicker or thinner; the uniformity and “bigger — faster” do not.)
bodies-in-liquids-answer-bubble-speed-text-3 = { $value2 }. Twice the path — twice the time: the path is proportional to the time, and l/t is the same at every mark (a spread of { $value } % — the stopwatch shows only hundredths of a second). So the motion is uniform: v ≈ { $v } and { $v2 }. The bigger bubble rose faster — ≈ { $ratio } times.{ $value3 }
