bodies-in-liquids-diameter-options-2 = 1.5 mm
bodies-in-liquids-answer-ball-why = The ball sinks steadily when its weight is balanced by the buoyant force and the liquid’s drag. The liquid’s density changes only the buoyant force — and for a steel ball 6–8 times as dense as these liquids it is small. What matters is the viscosity, the liquid’s “thickness”: the higher it is, the harder the liquid brakes the ball. Glycerol is over a thousand times as viscous as water, oil some fifty. So in oil, the lightest of the three, the ball sinks slower than in water, and in glycerol, the densest, hardly at all.
